A NULL colour space must answer, not crash.

Three C accessors in O2ColorSpace read an ivar straight out of their argument with no NULL check.
Two of them are reached with NULL by a path this port has always had, and iA Writer dies there:
opening Preferences and clicking the Editor pane is a SIGSEGV, three of three before this and four
of four counting a run with eager binding.

The chain, measured rather than reasoned:

    +[NSColor colorWithCGColor:]  guards the COLOUR and not its colour space:

        if (cgColor == NULL)
            return nil;
        switch (CGColorSpaceGetModel(CGColorGetColorSpace(cgColor))) {

    CGColorGetColorSpace answers NULL, CGColorSpaceGetModel is O2ColorSpaceGetModel, and that is
    "return self->_type" with nothing in front of it. The faulting instruction is the load itself,
    movl 0x8(%rdi), %eax, at O2ColorSpaceGetModel+4.

CoreGraphics answers kCGColorSpaceModelUnknown, which is -1, for a NULL colour space, and this
enum has no member for it, so the cast is deliberate. Minus one sends the switch above to its
default, which picks NSDeviceRGBColorSpace. O2ColorSpaceGetNumberOfComponents answers 0, which is
also what CoreGraphics does.

After this the Editor pane opens and renders: text size, typeface, typography, line length limit,
and the seven highlight colour swatches, which are the colours that needed a colour space at all.
The guards fire 20 times each in one drive, so the NULL is common on that path rather than an
edge.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX, and it is the real gap: something is creating a CGColor with no colour
space. O2ColorCreate is reached with a NULL colour space too, at O2ColorCreate+60. The trace here,
behind CIDER_TRACE_COLORSPACE, prints the return address of whoever passes NULL so that gap can be
found next. This change stops an application dying four layers from the cause; it does not close
the cause.

O2ColorSpaceIsPlatformRGB is the third accessor with the same shape. Nothing reaches it with NULL
today and this does not claim otherwise; it is guarded because its two neighbours both were.

--- a/Onyx2D/O2ColorSpace.m
+++ b/Onyx2D/O2ColorSpace.m
@@ -51,6 +51,9 @@
 const CFStringRef kO2ColorSpaceExtendedLinearGray =
         CFSTR("kCGColorSpaceExtendedLinearGray");
 
+#import <stdlib.h>
+#import <stdio.h>
+
 @implementation O2ColorSpace
 
 - initWithDeviceGray {
@@ -182,10 +185,26 @@
 }
 
 BOOL O2ColorSpaceIsPlatformRGB(O2ColorSpaceRef self) {
+    /* The third of these accessors to read an ivar out of a colour space that may be NULL. Nothing
+     * reaches this one with NULL today; it is guarded because its two neighbours both did. */
+    if (self == NULL)
+        return NO;
+
     return self->_isPlatformRGB;
 }
 
 size_t O2ColorSpaceGetNumberOfComponents(O2ColorSpaceRef self) {
+    /* A NULL COLOUR SPACE IS A MISSING ANSWER FROM SOMEWHERE ELSE, and reading _type out of it
+     * turns that into a segfault with no connection to the cause. CoreGraphics answers 0. The
+     * trace names the caller so the real gap is still findable. */
+    if (self == NULL) {
+        if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_COLORSPACE") != NULL) {
+            fprintf(stderr, "CIDER_CSNULL components ret=%p\n", __builtin_return_address(0));
+            fflush(stderr);
+        }
+        return 0;
+    }
+
     switch (self->_type) {
 
     case kO2ColorSpaceModelMonochrome:
@@ -209,6 +228,18 @@
 }
 
 O2ColorSpaceModel O2ColorSpaceGetModel(O2ColorSpaceRef self) {
+    /* CoreGraphics answers kCGColorSpaceModelUnknown, which is -1, and this enum has no member for
+     * it. +[NSColor colorWithCGColor:] guards the colour but not its colour space, so a NULL one
+     * reached here and killed iA Writer opening the Editor preferences pane; -1 sends that switch
+     * to its default instead. */
+    if (self == NULL) {
+        if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_COLORSPACE") != NULL) {
+            fprintf(stderr, "CIDER_CSNULL model ret=%p\n", __builtin_return_address(0));
+            fflush(stderr);
+        }
+        return (O2ColorSpaceModel) -1;
+    }
+
     return self->_type;
 }
 
